        },
        {
          "concern": "The diagnostic prints an always-true Boolean instead of the scopes missing from the grant.",
          "response": "Inline1217892 proposes making deltaScope an array of missing scopes and logging it. The request is Done without a rendered response explaining why the literal array approach was not used.",
          "applied": "Final code keeps deltaScope as the Boolean result of some. The warning prints the requested and granted scope strings and ends with a period; it does not implement the literal missing-scope-array suggestion. The receiver is the token array returned by split, not String.includes.",
          "outcome": "The diagnostic now includes actual scope strings. Badge state and literal source application remain separate; there is no new array mandate or inferred lint/test run."
        },
        {
          "concern": "Saving an unchanged token string can still require changing its stored scope realm; a caller gate can prevent reaching that helper.",
          "response": "The review supplies O365 restart and Google granular-permission instructions plus a Try link. Those are instructions and a locator, not executed results by B. Main bug reports are retained separately.",
          "applied": "The selected setRefreshToken searches by origin and skips another username, then updates password and httpRealm independently. It removes a matching login for an empty token and writes server scope preferences whenever _prefRoot exists. However, the selected fetch caller still invokes the helper only when the token string changes. Frozen code also invokes it for scope changes, updates _scope, checks grant-set coverage and avoids no-op writes.",
          "outcome": "The helper improvement does not prove historical scope-only calls reached it. Reuse the existing current persistence contract and historical caller-gate proposal without creating a duplicate rule. No stored-login mutation, provider flow, prompt teardown or restart test ran."
        },
        {
          "concern": "The intermediate fix and the later normalization have distinct provider, profile and release outcomes.",
          "response": "The main bug starts with Office365 SMTP timeout and duplicate-login errors. The author initially proposes dropping offline_access; reviewer comments6/10 qualify and reconsider its refresh-token result. Later QA reports successful restart/Google paths but an EWS Sent-copy failure, while an IMAP-only new-profile report works. The original reporter later succeeds after clearing a stale server oauth2.scope preference. Upgrade-from128 verification is a historical QA report.",
          "applied": "The selected659 token membership fix survives, but674 restores offline_access and normalizes only Microsoft token results. Old-profile state and provider/transport differences cannot be collapsed into one source mechanism. ESR approval/landing and128.2.3 backout are separate from central FIXED status.",
